feat(runs): the corpus table, once a real page had had a look at it

Two things the built page showed that the markup could not.

Astro strips the whitespace between sibling elements, so the date, the
repo key and the status - three inline spans with `nowrap` on them - had
no break opportunity anywhere between them. The row header's *minimum*
width became the whole line set as one unbreakable word, and it pushed
`spent` and `gates green` off the side of the table. The meta line is a
flex row now, whose items wrap between themselves; the status moves up
beside the sentence, which is where EvidenceStrip.astro already puts it.

And the reading measure is the wrong measure for a table. 62-72ch is how
far an eye tracks a line, and this is read across: inside the column the
table overflowed a 1280px desktop window and handed every reader the
scrollbar that was meant for the phone. It breaks out by --space-3xl
either side above 64rem - the breakpoint the sticky table of contents
already uses, because a site with one breakpoint should not grow a second
one nobody can explain. Below it the scroll region does the work, and it
is focusable and labelled so a keyboard can still reach the columns.

Measured after: scrollWidth 752 === clientWidth 752, no horizontal
overflow on the document at 1280 or at 504. Screenshots of both schemes
and the rest of the proof in docs/evidence/S6.4-the-corpus-index.md.

Stage S6 complete. Battery: 0 errors, 86 tests, build exit 0 with 478
annotations on 21 pages, evidence and anonymity green.
